{"name":"Tie clip / tie bar","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Clip-On_Tie.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clip-On_Tie.jpg","caption":"Clip-On Tie","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A tie clip, also called a tie bar or tie clasp, holds a necktie against the shirt front so it does not swing or flip around. It looks like a small decorative bar across the tie.","The payoff is a tidier, more finished formal look. It became popular in the 1920s with thin silk ties and remains a menswear accessory; there is no research on it as an appearance tool."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Wearers clip it across the tie and shirt placket. Grokipedia describes the usual position as a few inches below the knot, and clips come in metals such as silver, gold or brass."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No health risks; a clip can mark delicate fabric if worn too tight, and overly ornate designs may look out of place in conservative settings."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Clip-On_Tie.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Clip-On_Tie.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Clip-On Tie","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Dep._Garcia (talk) (Uploads)","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clip-On_Tie.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/2_silver_tie_clips.JPG/960px-2_silver_tie_clips.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/2_silver_tie_clips.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campa